<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Assignment 3: Convergence of Taylor Series, the Binomial Series and
  Applications.

  Problems drawn from the old "Exercises for Taylor Series and its
  convergence" and "Exercises for The Binomial Series" worksheets.
  exer-dipole-tolerance is starred: optional, not examined.

  Everything here that uses the binomial series carries component="binomial":
  the student-facing publication files keep only the web/print components, so
  PreTeXt drops it from the student books, while the instructor books list
  "binomial" in their <version include> and keep it.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 3</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems bound the error in a Taylor approximation with the Remainder
Theorem and use Taylor series to compute limits and integrals.
Problems marked with a star (<m>*</m>) are for the interested reader: they do
not need to be handed in and will not be examined. If you would like to review the material first, see
<xref ref="section-taylor" text="title"/>.
</p>
<p component="binomial">
The starred problems at the end use the binomial series to approximate a
physical law; for those, see
<xref ref="chapter-binomial-series" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-6">
<statement>
<p>
For approximately what values of <m>x</m> can we replace
<m>\sin(x)</m> by <m>x - \dfrac{x^3}{6}</m> with an error of magnitude
no greater than <m>4 \times 10^{-3}</m>?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here <m>a = 0</m>, and the cubic <m>x - \dfrac{x^3}{3!}</m> is
<m>T_3(x)</m>. But the Maclaurin series of <m>\sin</m> has no
<m>x^4</m> term, so the same polynomial is also the fourth order
Taylor polynomial:
<md>
<mrow>T_4(x) \amp = x - \frac{x^3}{3!} + \frac{f^{(4)}(0)}{4!}x^4</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = x - \frac{x^3}{3!} = T_3(x),
\qquad\text{since } f^{(4)}(0) = \sin(0) = 0 .</mrow>
</md>
We should therefore take <m>n = 4</m>, not <m>n = 3</m>: both choices
describe the very same approximation, but the remainder <m>R_4</m> is
one order smaller than <m>R_3</m> and so gives the sharper estimate.
</p>

<p>
The derivatives of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> are
<md>
<mrow>f'(x) \amp = \cos(x), \amp
f''(x) \amp = -\sin(x),</mrow>
<mrow>f^{(3)}(x) \amp = -\cos(x), \amp
f^{(4)}(x) \amp = \sin(x),</mrow>
<mrow>f^{(5)}(x) \amp = \cos(x), \amp \amp</mrow>
</md>
and therefore
<m>\left|f^{(5)}(c)\right| = |\cos(c)| \le 1</m>, so we can take
<m>M = 1</m>.
</p>

<p>
By the Remainder Theorem with <m>n = 4</m>,
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_4(x)| \le M\,\frac{|x-a|^5}{5!}
= \frac{|x|^5}{5!} \le 4\times 10^{-3},
</md>
which gives
<md>
<mrow>|x| \amp \le \sqrt[5]{(5!)(0.004)} = \sqrt[5]{0.48} \approx 0.863,</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \Rightarrow\quad x \in [-0.863,\; 0.863].</mrow>
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-rev-sin-approx"/>.
</p>

<p>
For comparison, stopping at <m>n = 3</m> would have given
<m>|x| \le \sqrt[4]{(4!)(0.004)} \approx 0.556</m>, a needlessly small
interval for exactly the same polynomial.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-sin-approx">
<caption>
The function <m>y = \sin x</m> and the cubic <m>y = x - \frac{x^3}{6}</m>.
Between the dashed lines <m>x = \pm 0.863</m> the two graphs differ by
at most <m>4\times 10^{-3}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="78%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rev-sin-approx">
<diagram dimensions="(400,270)" margins="12">
<definition>f(t) = (t, sin(t))</definition>
<definition>p(t) = (t, t - t^3/6)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.7,-1.7,2.7,1.9)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="sa-left" endpoints="((-0.863,-1.45),(-0.863,1.45))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="sa-right" endpoints="((0.863,-1.45),(0.863,1.45))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="sa-p" function="p" domain="(-2.6,2.6)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="sa-f" function="f" domain="(-2.6,2.6)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" dash="7 4"/>
