</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-7">
<statement>
<p>
Use the remainder estimation theorem to estimate the maximum error when
approximating <m>f(x) = e^x</m> by <m>T_2(x) = 1 + x + \dfrac{x^2}{2}</m> on the
interval <m>\left[-\dfrac56, \dfrac56\right]</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
From the remainder theorem, we know that
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_2(x)|
= \left|\frac{f^{(3)}(c)}{3!}\,x^3\right|
\le \frac{e^c}{3!}\,|x|^3.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Also, since <m>c \in \left[-\frac56, \frac56\right]</m>, we have
<m>|c| \le \frac56</m> and hence <m>e^c \le e^{5/6}</m>:
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_2(x)|
\le \frac{e^{5/6}}{3!}\left(\frac56\right)^{3}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
We know that <m>e^{5/6} \lt e^1 \lt 3</m>, hence
<md>
\text{Error} = |R_2(x)|
\le \frac{e^{5/6}}{3!}\left(\frac56\right)^{3}
\lt \frac{3}{3!}\left(\frac56\right)^{3} \approx 0.289.
</md>
Hence, an estimate for the upper bound of the error is
<m>\dfrac{3}{3!}\left(\dfrac56\right)^{3} \approx 0.289</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rev-exp-approx"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-exp-approx">
<caption>
The function <m>y = e^x</m> and <m>y = T_2(x) = 1 + x + \tfrac{x^2}{2}</m> on the
interval <m>\left[-\frac56, \frac56\right]</m>, marked by the dashed
lines.
</caption>
<image width="76%">
