</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m1-4">
<statement>
<p>
Let
<md>
<mrow>f(x) \amp = 1 + 2x + 3x^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + 4x^3 + 5x^4 + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
Find the exact value of <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{1/13} f(x)\,dx</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First we identify <m>f</m>. Its general term is <m>(n+1)x^n</m>, so
<md>
f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (n+1)x^n .
</md>
This is exactly what you get by differentiating the geometric series
term by term:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{1}{1-x} \amp = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} x^n
  = 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + \cdots , \qquad |x| \lt 1,</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{1}{1-x}\right)
  \amp = \frac{1}{(1-x)^2}
  = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n x^{n-1}
  = 1 + 2x + 3x^2 + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
So <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{(1-x)^2}</m> for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>. Since
<m>\tfrac1{13} \lt 1</m>, the whole interval of integration lies inside
the interval of convergence.
</p>

<p>
Now we integrate. An antiderivative of <m>(1-x)^{-2}</m> is
<m>(1-x)^{-1}</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>\int_0^{1/13} \frac{dx}{(1-x)^2}
  \amp = \left[\frac{1}{1-x}\right]_0^{1/13}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{1-\frac{1}{13}} - \frac{1}{1-0}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{\frac{12}{13}} - 1
   = \frac{13}{12} - 1 = \frac{1}{12} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-integral-cos-over-x">
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\displaystyle\int \frac{\cos x - 1}{x}\,dx</m> as an infinite
series. Give the first three nonzero terms.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The integrand has no elementary antiderivative, so expand it as a series
first. Starting from
<m>\cos x = 1 - \dfrac{x^2}{2!} + \dfrac{x^4}{4!}
- \dfrac{x^6}{6!} + \cdots</m>, subtracting <m>1</m> kills the constant
term, so every surviving power of <m>x</m> is even and at least
<m>2</m>. Dividing by <m>x</m> therefore leaves an honest power series:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\cos x - 1}{x}
\amp = \frac{1}{x}\left(-\frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{x^4}{4!}
  - \frac{x^6}{6!} + \cdots\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -\frac{x}{2!} + \frac{x^3}{4!} - \frac{x^5}{6!} + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Integrating term by term,
<md>
<mrow>\int \frac{\cos x - 1}{x}\,dx
\amp = C - \frac{x^2}{2\cdot 2!} + \frac{x^4}{4\cdot 4!}
  - \frac{x^6}{6\cdot 6!} + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = C - \frac{x^2}{4} + \frac{x^4}{96}
  - \frac{x^6}{4320} + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-series-limit-cos2x">
<statement>
<p>
Use Taylor series to evaluate
<md>
\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{\cos(2x) - 1}{x^2 e^{x}} .
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The quotient has the indeterminate form <m>\frac00</m>. Replace the
numerator and the denominator by their Maclaurin series. Putting
<m>2x</m> in place of <m>x</m> in
<m>\cos x = 1 - \frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{x^4}{4!} - \cdots</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\cos(2x) \amp = 1 - \frac{(2x)^2}{2!} + \frac{(2x)^4}{4!} - \cdots
  = 1 - 2x^2 + \frac{2}{3}x^4 - \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\Rightarrow\quad \cos(2x) - 1
  \amp = -2x^2 + \frac{2}{3}x^4 - \cdots</mrow>
</md>
and from <m>e^{x} = 1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2!} + \cdots</m>,
<md>
x^2 e^{x} = x^2\left(1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2!} + \cdots\right)
= x^2 + x^3 + \frac{x^4}{2} + \cdots .
</md>
</p>
<p>
Both vanish to exactly second order, so cancel the common factor of
<m>x^2</m> <mdash/> legitimate, since the limit never uses the value at
<m>x = 0</m> itself:
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{\cos(2x) - 1}{x^2 e^{x}}
\amp = \lim_{x\to 0}
  \frac{x^2\left(-2 + \frac{2}{3}x^2 - \cdots\right)}
       {x^2\left(1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2!} + \cdots\right)}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \lim_{x\to 0}
  \frac{-2 + \frac{2}{3}x^2 - \cdots}
       {1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2!} + \cdots}
  = \frac{-2}{1} = -2 .</mrow>
</md>
Each remaining series is continuous at <m>0</m> with a nonzero value
there, so only the two leading coefficients matter.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-integral-sin-ln">
<statement>
<p>
Show that
<md>
\int_0^1 \frac{\sin(\ln t)}{\ln t}\,dt = \frac{\pi}{4}.
</md>
(The integrand is continuous on <m>(0,1)</m>. As <m>t \to 1^-</m> we have
<m>\ln t \to 0</m>, so the integrand tends to <m>1</m>; as <m>t \to 0^+</m>
we have <m>\left|\dfrac{\sin(\ln t)}{\ln t}\right| \le \dfrac{1}{|\ln t|} \to 0</m>.
So the integrand is bounded and the integral exists. Its antiderivative is
not elementary.)
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
Two facts from Calculus II, both proved by integration by parts<fn>For
<m>I_k</m>: <m>I_0 = \int_0^{\infty} e^{-x}\,dx = 1</m>, and for
<m>k \ge 1</m> integration by parts with <m>u = x^k</m> and
<m>dv = e^{-x}\,dx</m> gives
<m>I_k = \Big[-x^k e^{-x}\Big]_0^{\infty} + k\,I_{k-1} = k\,I_{k-1}</m>, since
<m>x^k e^{-x} \to 0</m> as <m>x \to \infty</m>; by
induction <m>I_k = k!</m>. For <m>J_k</m>: <m>J_0 = 1</m>, and for
<m>k \ge 1</m> integration by parts with <m>u = (\ln t)^k</m> and
<m>dv = dt</m>, so <m>du = k(\ln t)^{k-1}\,\frac{dt}{t}</m> and <m>v = t</m>,
gives <m>J_k = \Big[t(\ln t)^k\Big]_0^1 - k\,J_{k-1} = -k\,J_{k-1}</m>. The
bracket vanishes at <m>t = 1</m> because
<m>\ln 1 = 0</m>, and at <m>t = 0^+</m> because <m>t(\ln t)^k \to 0</m> (by
L'Hôpital's rule). By induction,
<m>J_k = (-k)(-(k-1))\cdots(-1)\,J_0 = (-1)^k k!</m>.</fn>: for every
integer <m>k \ge 0</m>,
<md>
<mrow>I_k \amp = \int_0^{\infty} x^k e^{-x}\,dx = k!,</mrow>
<mrow>J_k \amp = \int_0^1 (\ln t)^k\,dt = (-1)^k\,k!.</mrow>
</md>
Either substitute <m>t = e^{-x}</m> and use <xref ref="ex-taylor-integral-exp-sin"/>, or
expand <m>\dfrac{\sin(\ln t)}{\ln t}</m> as a series in <m>\ln t</m> and
integrate term by term with <m>J_k</m>.
</p>
</hint>
<solution>
<p>
<em>Solution 1 (substitution).</em> Let <m>t = e^{-x}</m>, so
<m>x = -\ln t</m> and <m>dt = -e^{-x}\,dx</m>. As <m>t</m> runs from
<m>0</m> to <m>1</m>, <m>x</m> runs from <m>\infty</m> to <m>0</m>. Since
<m>\ln t = -x</m>,
<md>
\frac{\sin(\ln t)}{\ln t} = \frac{\sin(-x)}{-x} = \frac{\sin x}{x}.
</md>
Therefore
<md>
<mrow>\int_0^1 \frac{\sin(\ln t)}{\ln t}\,dt
\amp = \int_{\infty}^{0} \frac{\sin x}{x}\,\left(-e^{-x}\right)dx</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \int_0^{\infty} e^{-x}\,\frac{\sin x}{x}\,dx = \frac{\pi}{4}</mrow>
</md>
by <xref ref="ex-taylor-integral-exp-sin"/>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution 2 (directly with series).</em> Substituting <m>u = \ln t</m>
into the series
<m>\dfrac{\sin u}{u} = \displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n u^{2n}}{(2n+1)!}</m>,
<md>
\frac{\sin(\ln t)}{\ln t} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n (\ln t)^{2n}}{(2n+1)!}.
</md>
Integrating term by term and using <m>J_k</m> with <m>k = 2n</m> (so
<m>(-1)^{2n} = 1</m>),
<md>
<mrow>\int_0^1 \frac{\sin(\ln t)}{\ln t}\,dt
\amp = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n}{(2n+1)!}\,(2n)!</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n}{2n+1}
= \arctan 1 = \frac{\pi}{4}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<em>Justification.</em> With
<m>S_N(x) = \displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{N} \frac{(-1)^n x^{2n}}{(2n+1)!}</m>, the
Remainder Estimation Theorem gives
<m>\left|\dfrac{\sin x}{x} - S_N(x)\right| \le \dfrac{x^{2N+2}}{(2N+3)!}</m>,
exactly as in <xref ref="ex-taylor-integral-exp-sin"/>. Replacing <m>x</m> by
<m>u = \ln t</m>,
<md>
\left|\frac{\sin(\ln t)}{\ln t} - S_N(\ln t)\right| \le \frac{(\ln t)^{2N+2}}{(2N+3)!}.
</md>
Integrating over <m>[0,1]</m> with <m>J_k</m>, <m>k = 2N+2</m>, bounds the
error after <m>N+1</m> terms by
<md>
\frac{(2N+2)!}{(2N+3)!} = \frac{1}{2N+3} \longrightarrow 0,
</md>
so the partial sums converge to the integral, and the integral equals
<m>\pi/4</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-dipole-field" component="binomial">
    <title>*Approximating The Electric Field due to an Electric Dipole</title>
    <statement>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
        <p>Consider an electric dipole consisting of two charges, <m>+q</m> and <m>-q</m>, 
          separated by a distance <m>d</m>. The electric field at a point <m>P</m> located at a distance <m>r</m>
          from the positive charge along the axis of the dipole is given by:
<md>
          E = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( \frac{q}{r^2} - \frac{q}{(r+d)^2} \right)
        </md>
</p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-dipole-schematic">
  <caption>An electric dipole: charges <m>+q</m> and <m>-q</m> separated by a
  distance <m>d</m>, with the field point <m>P</m> a distance <m>r</m> from
  the positive charge along the axis.</caption>
  <image width="70%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="dipole-schematic">
      <diagram dimensions="(460,200)" margins="8">
        <!-- Dipole schematic: -q at x=-1.1, +q at x=0, point P at x=3.2 on the axis.
             Distances: d (between charges), r (from +q to P). -->
        <definition>xneg = -1.1</definition>
        <definition>xpos = 0</definition>
        <definition>xP = 3.2</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-1.9, -1.0, 4.0, 1.2)" aspect-ratio="1">
          <line at="axis" p1="(-1.7, 0)" p2="(3.8, 0)" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
          <group at="negcharge">
            <point p="(xneg, 0)" fill="blue" size="13"/>
            <label anchor="(xneg, 0)" alignment="c" color="white"><m>-q</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="poscharge">
            <point p="(xpos, 0)" fill="red" size="13"/>
            <label anchor="(xpos, 0)" alignment="c" color="white"><m>+q</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="pointP">
            <point p="(xP, 0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
            <label anchor="(xP, 0)" alignment="ne" offset="(2,4)"><m>P</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="ddist">
            <line p1="(xneg, 0.42)" p2="(xpos, 0.42)" stroke="black" thickness="1" arrows="2"/>
            <line p1="(xneg, 0)" p2="(xneg, 0.5)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <line p1="(xpos, 0)" p2="(xpos, 0.5)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5*(xneg+xpos), 0.42)" alignment="n" offset="(0,3)"><m>d</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="rdist">
            <line p1="(xpos, -0.42)" p2="(xP, -0.42)" stroke="black" thickness="1" arrows="2"/>
            <line p1="(xpos, 0)" p2="(xpos, -0.5)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <line p1="(xP, 0)" p2="(xP, -0.5)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5*(xpos+xP), -0.42)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-3)"><m>r</m></label>
          </group>
        </coordinates>
        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="axis"
              text="A schematic of an electric dipole on a horizontal axis. A negative charge minus q and a positive charge plus q are separated by a distance d. A point P lies on the axis a distance r to the right of the positive charge" >
            <annotation ref="negcharge" text="The negative charge, minus q, on the left"/>
            <annotation ref="poscharge" text="The positive charge, plus q, a distance d to the right of the negative charge"/>
            <annotation ref="ddist" text="The separation d between the two charges"/>
            <annotation ref="pointP" text="The point P on the axis, to the right of the positive charge"/>
            <annotation ref="rdist" text="The distance r from the positive charge to the point P"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>
        <p>
          Use the Taylor series to approximate the electric field <m>E</m> at point <m>P</m> 
          for <m>d \ll r</m>. Show that the leading term in the approximation is proportional to 
          <m>d/r^3</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>To approximate the electric field <m>E</m> at point <m>P</m> 
           for <m>d \ll r</m>, we can use the Taylor series expansion for 
           the function <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{(r+x)^2}</m> around <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>We have:
<md>
          f(x) = \frac{1}{(r+x)^2} = \frac{1}{r^2} \left( 1 + \frac{x}{r} \right)^{-2}
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Using the binomial series expansion, we can write:
<md>
          \left( 1 + \frac{x}{r} \right)^{-2} = 1 - 2\frac{x}{r} + 3\frac{x^2}{r^2} - 4\frac{x^3}{r^3} + \cdots
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Substituting <m>x=d</m> and <m>x=0</m>, we get:
<md>
          f(d) = \frac{1}{(r+d)^2} = \frac{1}{r^2} \left( 1 - 2\frac{d}{r} + 3\frac{d^2}{r^2} - 4\frac{d^3}{r^3} + \cdots \right)
        </md>
<md>
          f(0) = \frac{1}{r^2}
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Now, we can write the electric field <m>E</m> as:
<md>
          <mrow>E \amp = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( \frac{q}{r^2} - \frac{q}{(r+d)^2} \right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( \frac{1}{r^2} - \frac{1}{(r+d)^2} \right)</mrow>
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Substituting the Taylor series expansion for <m>f(d)</m> and <m>f(0)</m>, we get:
<md>
          E = \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( \frac{1}{r^2} - \frac{1}{r^2} \left( 1 - 2\frac{d}{r} + 3\frac{d^2}{r^2} - 4\frac{d^3}{r^3} + \cdots \right) \right)
        </md>
</p>
   
        <p>Simplifying, we have:
<md>
          E = \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \left( 2\frac{d}{r^3} - 3\frac{d^2}{r^4} + 4\frac{d^3}{r^5} - \cdots \right)
        </md>
</p>
        <p>Thus, the leading term in the approximation is:
<md>
          E \approx \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \cdot 2\frac{d}{r^3}
        </md>
which shows that the leading term is proportional to <m>d/r^3</m>.</p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-dipole-field">
  <caption>The axial field of a dipole, <m>E = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
  \left(\frac{q}{r^2} - \frac{q}{(r+d)^2}\right)</m>, compared with its
  leading Taylor term for <m>d \ll r</m>. The leading term
  <m>\frac{2qd}{4\pi\epsilon_0 r^3} \propto \frac{d}{r^3}</m> overshoots
  slightly at small <m>r</m> but converges to the exact field as <m>r</m>
  grows.</caption>
  <image width="65%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="dipole-field">
      <diagram dimensions="(420,320)" margins="8">
        <!-- Dipole axial field: E = k(q/r^2 - q/(r+d)^2). Natural units k=q=d=1.
             Leading Taylor term for d << r is 2qd/(4 pi eps0 r^3), proportional to d/r^3. -->
        <definition>Eexact(r) = 1/r^2 - 1/(r+1)^2</definition>
        <definition>Elead(r) = 2/r^3</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(1.15, -0.04, 6.4, 0.66)">
          <grid-axes xlabel="r" ylabel="E" hlabels="(2, 1, 6)" vlabels="(0.2, 0.2, 0.6)"/>
          <graph at="lead" function="Elead" domain="(1.5, 6)" stroke="red" thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
          <graph at="exact" function="Eexact" domain="(1.5, 6)" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
          <label at="leadlabel" anchor="(2.35, Elead(2.35))" alignment="ne" offset="(4,6)" color="red">
            <m>\dfrac{2qd}{4\pi\epsilon_0 r^3} \propto \dfrac{d}{r^3}</m>
          </label>
          <label at="exactlabel" anchor="(3.4, Eexact(3.4))" alignment="se" offset="(4,-6)" color="blue">
            <m>E = \dfrac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\!\left( \dfrac{q}{r^2} - \dfrac{q}{(r+d)^2} \right)</m>
          </label>
          <label at="regime" anchor="(5.2, 0.32)" alignment="c">
            <m>d \ll r</m>
          </label>
        </coordinates>
        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="exact"
              text="The axial electric field of a dipole as a function of distance r. The exact field, E equals the constant times q over r squared minus q over quantity r plus d squared, is shown together with its leading Taylor term for d much less than r, which is 2 q d over 4 pi epsilon naught r cubed and is proportional to d over r cubed. The two curves separate at small r but converge as r grows, showing that the leading term captures the far-field behavior">
            <annotation ref="exact" text="The exact dipole field, decreasing with distance" sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="lead" text="The leading Taylor term, proportional to d over r cubed, which overshoots slightly but approaches the exact field for large r" sonify="yes"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>
    </solution>   
    
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-dipole-tolerance" component="binomial">
  <title>*How small must the separation be?</title>
  <statement>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
    <p>
      A dipole is often treated as a <term>point dipole</term> by replacing
      the exact axial field
      <md>
        E = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left( \frac{q}{r^2} - \frac{q}{(r+d)^2} \right)
      </md>
      with its leading-order Taylor term for <m>d \ll r</m>,
      <md>
        E \approx \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\cdot\frac{2qd}{r^3}.
      </md>
      Suppose this approximation must agree with the exact field to within a
      relative error of <m>1\%</m>. If the field point <m>P</m> is
      <m>r = 3.0\ \text{cm}</m> from the positive charge, how small must the
      charge separation <m>d</m> be for the point-dipole approximation to be
      valid?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>
      The dominant error comes from the <em>next</em> term in the expansion.
      Using
      <m>E = \dfrac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left( \dfrac{2d}{r^3} -
      \dfrac{3d^2}{r^4} + \cdots \right)</m>, form the relative error of the
      leading term and keep only the largest contribution.
    </p>
  </hint>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>d \lesssim \dfrac{2}{3}(0.01)\,r \approx 0.020\ \text{cm} = 0.20\ \text{mm}.</m>
    </p>
  </answer>
  <solution>
    <p>
      From the Taylor expansion,
      <md>
        E = \frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left( \frac{2d}{r^3} - \frac{3d^2}{r^4}
        + \cdots \right),
      </md>
      the leading term is <m>E_{\text{lead}} = \dfrac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\cdot
      \dfrac{2d}{r^3}</m>, and the first neglected term is
      <m>\dfrac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\cdot\dfrac{3d^2}{r^4}</m>. The relative
      error of the approximation is therefore
      <md>
        \frac{\left| E_{\text{lead}} - E \right|}{E}
          \approx \frac{\dfrac{3d^2}{r^4}}{\dfrac{2d}{r^3}}
          = \frac{3}{2}\cdot\frac{d}{r}.
      </md>
      Notice that <m>q</m> and <m>\epsilon_0</m> cancel, so the relative error
      depends only on the ratio <m>d/r</m>. Requiring this to be at most
      <m>1\% = 0.01</m> gives
      <md>
        \frac{3}{2}\cdot\frac{d}{r} \le 0.01
        \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
        d \le \frac{2}{3}(0.01)\,r.
      </md>
      With <m>r = 3.0\ \text{cm}</m>,
      <md>
        d \le \frac{2}{3}(0.01)(3.0\ \text{cm}) = 0.020\ \text{cm} = 0.20\ \text{mm}.
      </md>
      So the point-dipole approximation is accurate to <m>1\%</m> only when the
      charges are separated by less than about <m>0.2\ \text{mm}</m> at this
      distance<mdash/>consistent with <xref ref="fig-dipole-field"/>, where the
      two curves visibly merge as <m>r</m> grows relative to <m>d</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-1" component="binomial">
<title>*A binomial series for a rational function</title>
<statement>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
<p>
Use a <term>binomial series</term> to represent the function
<m>f(x) = \dfrac{2x}{1-x}</m> as a series. Write your final answer in
sigma notation.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We start from the binomial series with <m>m = -1</m>:
<md>
\frac{1}{1-x} = (1-x)^{-1}
= \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \binom{-1}{k} (-x)^k.
</md>
The binomial coefficients are
<md>
<mrow>\binom{-1}{0} \amp = 1 = (-1)^0,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{-1}{1} \amp = -1 = (-1)^1,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{-1}{2} \amp = \frac{(-1)(-2)}{2!} = (-1)^2,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{-1}{3} \amp = \frac{(-1)(-2)(-3)}{3!} = (-1)^3,</mrow>
</md>
and in general <m>\dbinom{-1}{k} = (-1)^k</m>. Hence
<md>
\frac{1}{1-x}
= \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (-1)^k (-x)^k
= \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} x^k.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Therefore,
<md>
f(x) = 2x\,\frac{1}{1-x}
= 2x \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} x^k
= \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} 2x^{k+1}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-binomial-maclaurin" component="binomial">
<title>*Maclaurin series from the binomial series</title>
<statement>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
<p>
Use the binomial series <xref ref="eq-binomial-series"/> to obtain
the Maclaurin series for
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{1}{(1+x)^3}</m>. Write your answer in sigma notation,
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}(\;\cdots)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\sqrt[7]{1+x}</m>. Give the first four terms only.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Here <m>m = -3</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>\frac{1}{(1+x)^3} \amp = (1+x)^{-3}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 - 3x + \frac{(-3)(-4)}{2!}x^2
   + \frac{(-3)(-4)(-5)}{3!}x^3 + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 - 3x + 6x^2 - 10x^3 + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
The coefficient of <m>x^k</m> is
<md>
<mrow>\frac{(-3)(-4)\cdots(-3-k+1)}{k!}
\amp = (-1)^k\,\frac{3\cdot 4\cdots (k+2)}{k!}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (-1)^k\,\frac{(k+2)!}{2\,k!} ,</mrow>
</md>
so
<md>
<mrow>\frac{1}{(1+x)^3}
\amp = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (-1)^k \frac{(k+2)!}{2\,k!}\,x^k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (-1)^k \frac{(k+1)(k+2)}{2}\,x^k .</mrow>
</md>
As a check, <m>k = 0, 1, 2, 3</m> give the coefficients
<m>1, -3, 6, -10</m> found above.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Here <m>m = \frac17</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt[7]{1+x} \amp = (1+x)^{1/7}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + \frac17 x
  + \frac{\left(\frac17\right)\left(-\frac67\right)}{2!}x^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \qquad
  + \frac{\left(\frac17\right)\left(-\frac67\right)
          \left(-\frac{13}{7}\right)}{3!}x^3 + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + \frac{x}{7} - \frac{6}{7^2\,2!}x^2
  + \frac{(6)(13)}{7^3\,3!}x^3 + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + \frac{x}{7} - \frac{3}{49}x^2
  + \frac{13}{343}x^3 + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-binomial-cube-root" component="binomial">
<title>*Expanding a cube root</title>
<statement>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
<p>
Expand the quantity <m>\sqrt[3]{P+t}</m> about <m>0</m> in powers of
<m>\dfrac{t}{P}</m>. Give four nonzero terms.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The binomial series expands <m>(1+x)^m</m>, so first factor <m>P</m> out
of the radicand to produce a <m>1 + {}</m> something:
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt[3]{P+t}
\amp = \left(P + P\left(\frac{t}{P}\right)\right)^{1/3}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(P\left(1 + \frac{t}{P}\right)\right)^{1/3}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \sqrt[3]{P}\left(1 + \frac{t}{P}\right)^{1/3}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Now apply the binomial series with <m>m = \frac13</m> and
<m>x = \frac{t}{P}</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt[3]{P+t} \amp = \sqrt[3]{P}\,\Biggl(
  1 + \frac13\cdot\frac{t}{P}
  + \frac{\frac13\left(\frac13-1\right)}{2!}
    \left(\frac{t}{P}\right)^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \qquad
  + \frac{\frac13\left(\frac13-1\right)\left(\frac13-2\right)}{3!}
    \left(\frac{t}{P}\right)^3 + \cdots \Biggr)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \sqrt[3]{P}\left(
  1 + \frac13\,\frac{t}{P}
  - \frac19\left(\frac{t}{P}\right)^2
  + \frac{5}{81}\left(\frac{t}{P}\right)^3 + \cdots \right).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m1-6" component="binomial">
<title>*Gravity above the surface of the Earth</title>
<statement>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
<p>
The gravitational force exerted by the Earth on an object of mass
<m>m</m> at height <m>h</m> above the surface is
<md>
F = \frac{mgR^2}{(R+h)^2} ,
</md>
where <m>R</m> is the radius of the Earth and <m>g</m> is the
acceleration due to gravity at the surface.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Express <m>F</m> as a Maclaurin series in the variable <m>h/R</m>,
writing out the first four non-zero terms.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What approximation to <m>F</m> do you get by keeping only the first
term? When is it reasonable?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> The point of the problem is to get the expression into
the form <m>(1+u)^{p}</m> with <m>u = h/R</m>. Factor <m>R</m> out of
the denominator:
<md>
F = \frac{mgR^2}{(R+h)^2}
  = \frac{mgR^2}{R^2\left(1+\frac{h}{R}\right)^2}
  = mg\left(1+\frac{h}{R}\right)^{-2} .
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now use the binomial series
<m>\displaystyle (1+u)^{p} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\binom{p}{n}u^n</m>
with <m>p = -2</m>. The coefficients are
<md>
<mrow>\binom{-2}{0} \amp = 1,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{-2}{1} \amp = -2,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{-2}{2} \amp = \frac{(-2)(-3)}{2!} = 3,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{-2}{3} \amp = \frac{(-2)(-3)(-4)}{3!} = -4 ,</mrow>
</md>
and in general <m>\binom{-2}{n} = (-1)^n (n+1)</m>. Hence
<md>
(1+u)^{-2} = 1 - 2u + 3u^2 - 4u^3 + \cdots , \qquad |u| \lt 1,
</md>
and so
<md>
F = mg\left[1 - 2\left(\frac{h}{R}\right)
    + 3\left(\frac{h}{R}\right)^2
    - 4\left(\frac{h}{R}\right)^3 + \cdots\right] .
</md>
This is valid for <m>0 \le h \lt R</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> Keeping only the first term gives
<md>
F \approx mg ,
</md>
the familiar constant weight of an object near the surface of the
Earth. It is reasonable when <m>h/R</m> is very small, that is when the
object stays close to the surface: the first term we discard is
<m>-2mgh/R</m>, so the relative error is about <m>2h/R</m>. With
<m>R \approx 6371</m> km, even at <m>h = 10</m> km the relative error is
only about <m>0.3\%</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
